als McCarthy was a  consistent pain for him waist in the  first couple of years later a bill  Noland and the China first folks really  were troublesome part of the party for  him to deal with in in foreign affairs  particularly in Taiwan and China highway  system is a good example I think of him  really not being banned to a kind of  Republican orthodoxy on the other side  civil rights I found it difficult to  find a sort of progressive course in  civil rights I don't think he really  felt it was hard he ended up having  quite a good record on civil rights but  in some ways despite himself where do  you stand on the idea that the the 50s  were where the real movement happened in  civil rights not the six yeah I think  that's accurate in terms of I mean  obviously there were movements across  both decades but you know Brown versus  Board of Education obviously is 54 you  know the the Montgomery bus boycott the  rise of Martin Luther King all of that  for the most part is in the night  50 and Ike's contribution to that was  indirect but profound he appointed her a  Warren he appointed William Brennan both  the Supreme Court and of course these  big most you know memorable contribution  is in 1957 in the Little Rock crisis  since the subtext was civil rights it  had the effect of him being a champion  of civil rights like as a TV president  how much do you think of him as a sort  of modern image based president well is  it's the first presidency to run with a  jingle a modern TV sense that of course  the growth of television throughout the  nineteen fifties is it's explosive he's  the first president to really work with  the medium and you worked with an  advertising agency in 52 and again in 56  to make the most out of his image he  didn't have the television presence  obviously the Kennedy data but he  understood its its potential and I think  it was the first to really take  advantage of it in that sense  I can the federal spending with the  caveat that he passed th